# =============================================================================
# JE SALES-INVOICE / CREDIT-MEMO MATCHING PIPELINE
# =============================================================================
# WHAT THIS NOTEBOOK DOES, END TO END
# -----------------------------------
# Input : JE_raw (current-quarter journal entries) + JE_raw_2_aligned
#         (prior-year carry-forward journal entries, same schema).
# Output: one Excel workbook containing every row from the input, sorted
#         into either a matched-pair sheet or an unmatched/exception sheet,
#         plus one parquet table per intermediate dataset for audit trail.
#
# The core problem: for each (Company, Vendor, |Amount|) combination, pair
# up "Sales Invoices" rows with "Credit Memos" rows that cancel each other
# out (net amount == 0), across four passes of increasing looseness:
#
#   PIPELINE 1 (Cell 4) — match within the current quarter's own data only.
#   PIPELINE 2 (Cell 5) — take what P1 couldn't match, add in prior-year
#                          carry-forward rows, and match again (this is the
#                          only pipeline that tags CY/PY on the pair).
#   PIPELINE 3 (Cell 5B) — take what P2 couldn't match, keep the exact-
#                          amount join key, but loosen month_lag to
#                          "greater than 2 months" (no upper bound). PKs
#                          whose sales/credit counts don't balance are
#                          pulled out before matching and sent straight to
#                          unmatched.
#   PIPELINE 4 (Cell 6) — take what P3 couldn't match, drop the exact-amount
#                          requirement from the join key, and allow up to
#                          $100 of difference (tolerance match).
#
# Every row that survives all four passes unmatched gets a rule-based
# "AA Comment" (Cell 7) explaining *why* it's unmatched, then everything
# — matched and unmatched — is written to one final .xlsx (Cell 8).
#
# ROW CONSERVATION is the invariant the whole notebook is built around:
# every row that goes into base_tag() must come back out somewhere in the
# final export. Cell 8 asserts this explicitly; Pipeline 4 asserts it
# per-pipeline (cnt_in == cnt_out).
# =============================================================================

